// check: refuse E_InitPerformsOwnOp
// The state init's sibling carrier: a handler's config DEFAULT performing an
// op that handler alone answers. A default and a state init both run where
// the install is made, before the install exists, so the judgment reads both
// at the declaration and refuses each the same way; this fixture holds the
// default's half true beside the init's (mn-refuse-init-performs-own-op).
effect F { g() -> Int }
handler hf(k: Int = g()) with s = 1 {
  g() => resume(k),
}
fn main(q) = (g()) ~> hf
